  data:
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 391f79cd63f7f3c48a05d57f9cb3bb7b, type: 3}
    titleName: 'Installing Operating System and Device Driver

'
    description: "Introduction\r\n \r\nWhen you open a computer, usually this will
      either be a Windows PC or a Macintosh computer. They will have different user
      interfaces or applications that may be specific to that computer. The Windows
      or the Mac are called operating systems.\r\n \r\nThe Operating System (OS)
      is a layer of software which is used to manage computer resources. It also
      provides an interface, so that users can utilize computer resources. It configures
      and manages hardware to connect the components and the applications.\r\n \r\n
      \r\nDifferent Operating System\r\n \r\nThe three most popular operating systems
      are Windows, MAC, and Linux.\r\n\n\n\xB7         Windows is the dominant over
      the other two. 90% of computer users use Windows operating system.\r\n\xB7        
      Windows and MAC are expensive operating systems however, MAC OS requires the
      users to buy the MAC systems (Macbook, Mac) built by Apple.\r\n\xB7        
      On the other hand, Linux is a free operating system.\r\n\xB7         Windows
      and MAC have similar file structures while Linux has a different code base
      (single file tree compared to Windows and MAC's many directories)\r\n\xB7        
      Windows interface has a Start menu, Taskbar, System Tray, and the Windows Explore
      while Linux has easy to switch interfaces.\r\n\xB7         Linux is most preferred
      by programmers, MAC is preferred by graphic artists, and Windows is perferred
      by gamers.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 2fda0dccea68124488ee38ea4bd9303d, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: "Common Operating Systems\r\n \r\nThe following are common operating
      systems being used today:\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 31cc5a8721be6ba48a46231800a6de5e, type: 3}
    titleName: 'Steps to Install Windows 10 on a New Computer

'
    description: "1.Unbox and Assemble the Hardware\r\nCarefully unbox and assemble
      all components if your new computer requires manual assembly. Ensure that all
      cables are properly connected, and peripherals such as keyboard, mouse, and
      monitor are set up.\r\n2.Connect to Power and Internet\r\nPlug in your computer
      and connect to the internet if possible. A wired connection is preferable for
      faster and more reliable access during installation.\r\n3.Prepare Installation
      Media\r\nDownload the Windows 10 Media Creation Tool from the official Microsoft
      website on another computer.\r\nUse the tool to create a bootable USB drive
      or DVD with the Windows 10 installation files.\r\n4.Insert Installation Media\r\nInsert
      the bootable USB drive or DVD into the new computer\r\n \r\n5.Turn On the Computer
      and Enter BIOS/UEFI\r\nTurn on the computer and immediately press a key such
      as F2, F12, DEL, or ESC to enter the BIOS/UEFI setup. The specific key will
      depend on your motherboard manufacturer.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: b9c9dc1542620c34887c5acf6651b75a, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: '6.Configure Boot Order

      In the BIOS/UEFI settings, locate
      the boot order menu.

      Set the USB drive or DVD as the primary boot device.
      Save the changes and exit BIOS/UEFI

'
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 9f7aa07bca1f4a3459950cf699870345, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: " 7.Boot from Installation Media .The computer should now boot from
      the installation media. You may be prompted to press a key to confirm this
      action.\r\n8.Start Windows 10 Installation\r\nSelect your language, time, and
      keyboard preferences, then click \u201CNext.\u201D\r\nClick \u201CInstall Now\u201D
      to begin the installation process.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 52e42a7603aaee94d8fddb7fdb72712d, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: "9.Enter Product Key\r\nEnter the Windows 10 product key when prompted.
      If you don't have one, you can select \u201CI don\u2019t have a product key\u201D
      to continue. Windows will prompt for activation later.\r\n10.Accept License
      Terms\r\nReview and accept the Microsoft Software License Terms, then click
      \u201CNext.\u201D\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 6e5858ab8a866174d9e940a67506f6e8, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: "11.Choose Installation Type\r\nSelect \u201CCustom: Install Windows
      only (advanced)\u201D for a clean installation on a new system.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 6154f37f31acba14ca482d0e87707330, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: "12.Partition the Drive\r\nChoose the drive you want to install
      Windows on. Since this is a new computer, there may be no existing partitions.\r\nCreate
      a new partition for Windows by selecting the unallocated space and clicking
      \u201CNew.\u201D The installer will automatically create additional necessary
      partitions for system files.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: b1dccf36a1c7efc46b9cd520dbfa5db6, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: "13.Begin Installation\r\nClick \u201CNext\u201D to start the installation.
      Windows will copy files, install features, and complete the setup. This process
      may take some time, depending on your system's specifications.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 5b200368c65cb6f478268605374a735d, type: 3}
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: b16d779fa8070de4fb63b8ed32768f04, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: '14. Set up initial configuration of the operating system. Choose
      the right region.

'
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 7beeb51ca613ec24e8a06b2a1a564f09, type: 3}
    titleName: 'Installing Device Drivers

'
    description: "There are several ways to install device drivers for your newly
      installed components:\r\n1. Manufacturer\u2019s DVD Installer \u2013 Provided
      upon buying a computer, laptop, device\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 22447092045915540a45419367158b11, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: " 2. Driverpack Solution \u2013 A free tool which automatically
      finds proper drivers for a computer, then downloads and installs it without
      wizards or installation prompts"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 5a10b63f8a994a84e85a8db9e3288aa5, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: "3. Device Manager (Driver Update) \u2013 Can be accessed by clicking
      Start > Computer > Manage > Device Manager. From here, each component can have
      the option to install a driver through a CD or through searching automatically
      online."
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 9250c6366e656e143b4be74d87321d9b, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: "4. Download Online \u2013 Look for unit\u2019s Model Name and Number\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: e46c124be0e2c1b429419bd5e2d7a000, type: 3}
    titleName: 'Checking Device Driver Installation

'
    description: "After installation, you would want to see if you've installed the
      correct device drivers to avoid any issues that may hinder the component from
      running in its full performance.\r\n \r\nIn this simple procedure, we are looking
      if there drivers are already installed. If the device driver is installed,
      updating it through the device manager would prompt to say that the drivers
      are installed.\r\n \r\n1. Open the Control Panel.\r\n2. Select System and Security.\r\n3.
      Click System and select Device Manager from the left side.\r\n4. Click \u201CMonitor\u201D 
      and you will see that a genetic monitor is installed.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
